  "answer": "To reduce the risk of a reinforcement-learning agent being deployed while exhibiting harmful side-effects, it has been proposed to evaluate the agent on the SafeLife side-effect benchmark prior to deployment (https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.01217). This mechanism works by testing trained agents on a suite of benchmark environments, specifically a 26x26 toroidal gridworld that uses extended Game-of-Life rules to produce large, unpredictable effects that make side-effect tendencies measurable (https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.01217). The use of procedural level generation in these environments increases diversity, which prevents the agent from overfitting and learning brittle, non-general safety behaviors (https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.01217). Based on this evaluation, models are rejected or iterated upon if they register high earth-mover deviation scores, which occurs when an agent optimizing for raw reward disrupts neutral patterns in the environment (https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.01217)."
